VISUAL: The creator in a studio setting, holding various selfie sticks and camera mounts, demonstrating accessories.
AUDIO: 'incredibly helpful for filming in 360. So, make sure you hit subscribe and turn on those notifications if you want to receive that video and all the helpful information inside of it. What do I film when I\'m actually out there with these cameras? I\'m feeling completely'

[06:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'overwhelmed. I just have no idea where to actually put the camera when I\'m filming. I don\'t like my results. This technique will help solve that for you. By far, the number one way I\'m using this camera is on a selfie stick. It is'

[06:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a really powerful way for moving these 360 cameras through physical space. And that is the best part about a selfie stick is that it\'s attached to your hand. And your hand means you can move it to any physical position in relation'

[06:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the subject that you\'re filming to get some really awesome results. I developed a technique I call the 360 compass to help remind myself when I\'m out in the field to actually be moving the camera in a way that\'s going to get'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'me the final result I actually want to see on my phone and on my computer. Now, instead of just holding it out directly in front of you at 0°, my most typical position would actually be about 45° offset from the direction that I or the'

[07:00] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: An overhead view of the creator on a patterned rug, with a compass overlay showing "340° N", illustrating the 360 compass technique.
AUDIO: 'subject is traveling. And what I recommend for most people for getting a unique variety of shots is actually moving through different degrees on this compass to assemble different shot perspectives for the final way you want'

[07:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to edit it together. And what I like about this is that you can approach this from two different ways. You can hold the camera on one perspective for a while, actually just physically hold it steady there, and then move to the next'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'position. Or you can imagine floating the camera through different degrees on the compass, holding at different ones for a little bit, smoothly moving through the next and imagining that your camera is like a drone that is following'

[07:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you. And how would that actually fly around you in a smooth pattern so that way you can reframe it later and have this really beautiful camera move. I find this is a really helpful way to break up how you\'re thinking about'

[07:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually holding the stick as you\'re doing it is that instead of just like holding it out from you in any position, try to imagine what\'s happening in the background. What place could I put this camera that would show the background'

