AUDIO: 'proud of it. I\'d love for you to experience it. The link is in the description as well. It happens sometimes where I just mount the camera in one spot and record from that position all day and then later I go, why don\'t I have dynamic feeling'

[15:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'coverage? I could Levi told me I could refframe 10 shots for every single shot, but the stuff just feels boring and static after the third or fourth shot. And that\'s where the big advantage of these cameras is you can move them'

[15:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around and place them and mount them in really unique spots. And so there\'s an idea here that I want to introduce to you, which is the camera itself. You want to place it in relation to your subject matter. And not just in relation'

[15:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to your subject matter, but then in relation to your subject matter, and the background. So those three variables, the camera, the subject, the background, if you tweak them and experiment, you'

[15:59] [scene:result]
VISUAL: A 360 shot of a dune buggy driving in the desert, showing dynamic movement.
AUDIO: 'might realize that they have a drastically different feel. For example, if you\'re filming a bike and you have it mounted on your head, the distance from the ground you are traveling on is quite far. So, you might not feel the shot has'

[16:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the speed it felt to actually be doing the activity yourself. Where if you bring that camera down to let\'s say a chest height or a mouth mount, you might find that that perspective is a lot more immersive and kind of feels more what'

[16:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you were expecting the shot to look like. And the same thing goes for cars or motorcycles. Again, my instincts tell me place the camera high up so it sees everything. But it\'s really cool, in fact, to bring the camera way lower to the surface you\'re traveling on. And'

[16:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then you get a really good sense of the speed and the blur of the ground as it moves by, which is really exciting. So, just because we are filming in 360° does not mean we just place it one spot'

[16:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and just don\'t care. The goal is to get dynamic shots that feel exciting, that tell a good story, and you can use best later. So, help yourself out while you\'re recording. Spend a couple moments just thinking, "Hey, where is the most'

[17:04] [scene:result]
VISUAL: On-screen text "X5 Example of A Lower Angle" over a person riding an e-bike on a path, illustrating preferred lower angles.
AUDIO: 'unique spot I could place this camera while I\'m doing this activity, while I\'m capturing this memory?" And you\'re going to thank yourself a thousand times later when we go to review the footage. When we\'re mounting the camera, we want to lower it down more than at least my'

[17:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'initial reaction or tendency is. So, we might say, "Hey, we\'ve mounted it. Let\'s put it all the way up. Good to go. Often times, again, like we talked about, we want to have the camera outwards from'

[17:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the main subject we\'re trying to show in the shot. That might mean bringing the camera down more than you expect for getting the ideal perspective. And we\'re doing this to both reduce distortion in the horizon, but also ideally getting'

[17:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the best perspective that\'s right in the heart of the action and shows motion really well. One of the biggest mistakes I noticed in my own personal use cases, no matter how awesome the camera angle that I mounted with whatever accessory'

[17:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that I was using, if I got a great angle, I had this tendency to just want to leave it in that exact spot for the whole activity. Like this sequence I just showed you isn\'t that interesting shot to shot. But if we just added some'

[18:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'diversity of camera angles, now it starts to feel like we have a sequence that we can actually work with when we go to edit this stuff. So, how do I choose what these angles should be? In the same way that when we use a selfie'

[18:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stick, we want to move the camera to these different perspectives around the subject matter. This also applies if we\'re mounting the camera to objects using accessories. So, if I\'m filming something like a bike, I will mount the'

[18:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera to a shot that I think is going to look great, and then once I feel I have enough footage of that, I will move them out to a new spot. Sometimes these shots just delight you and they work out fantastic and other times they\'re kind'

